    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d157-a3">

                <pb facs="0323" n="207" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg207"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Subinclosure 2.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Diplomatic Circular No. 194.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-12-29">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 29, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">surplus claims funds.</hi>
                </p>
                <p>The Dean has the honour to circulate herewith copy of a note which he has
                    received from the Wai Chiao Pu respecting the disposal of the surplus of the
                    funds set aside for payment of foreign claims arising out of the Revolution of
                    1911.</p>
                <p>The matter is one which primarily concerns the five Legations interested in the
                    Reorganization Loan; but inasmuch as the note is addressed to Sir John Jordan as
                    Dean, and as the payment of the Revolution claims is a question that concerns
                    the whole Diplomatic Body, the Dean thinks it well to submit the note for the
                    consideration of all his honourable colleagues.</p>
                <p>Sir John Jordan himself has no objection to the procedure proposed by the Wai
                    Chiao Pu.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
